---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/specs';

// Build-time only: on Pages this is the spec repo's slug; locally a placeholder.
const repoSlug = process.env.GITHUB_REPOSITORY ?? '<owner>/<repo>';
---
<StarlightPage frontmatter={{ title: 'Drive an implementation' }}>
  <p>
    These specs are packaged for consumption — an implementation proves it
    satisfies them; it never redefines them. The same loop applies to every
    service; each service overview carries its own current pin.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Pin the released surface</h2>
  <p>
    A merge to the spec repo's main publishes each changed service as a
    lightweight git tag <code>&lt;service&gt;/v&lt;version&gt;</code>. The
    implementation records which surface it satisfies in a
    <code> contracts.lock</code> at its root:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`# contract pin - version is the specs release tag, sha its commit
version: <service>/v<version>
sha: <commit sha of that tag>`}</code></pre>

  <h2>2. Fetch read-only, never vendor</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>contracts:fetch</code> task sparse-checks-out
    <code> specs/&lt;service&gt;</code> at the pinned <em>sha</em> into a
    write-protected <code>.contracts/</code> directory. Every run re-fetches,
    so a local edit cannot survive — and the sha, not the tag, is what gets
    checked out, so a re-cut tag cannot silently change what you build
    against.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Bind the acceptance criteria, strict</h2>
  <p>
    The feature files run from <code>.contracts/</code> as-is — the specs own
    the sentences, the implementation owns the step definitions. Strict mode
    is mandatory: an unbound or pending scenario fails the build, because an
    unbound scenario is a spec obligation silently dropped.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Verify — the definition of done</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Contract tests</strong> —
      <code> task mocks:contract SERVICE=&lt;name&gt; REST_ENDPOINT=… ASYNC_ENDPOINT=…</code>
      holds the running implementation to the same messages and operations
      the Microcks mocks serve.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The bound scenario suite</strong> against the running implementation.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Schema fuzz</strong> —
      <code> uvx schemathesis run .contracts/specs/&lt;name&gt;/openapi/*.yaml --url …</code>
      for declared-but-unexampled paths.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Consumers (UIs, clients) build against the mocks the same way before an
    implementation even exists: <code>task mocks:load</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Stay converged</h2>
  <p>
    The specs never push work at implementations; they pull. When a new
    release tag lands, Renovate opens a pin-bump PR on the implementation and
    the gates above run against the new surface. Green — typical for additive
    minors — auto-merges. Red, or a major bump, means the gates have proven
    code changes are needed, and the failing checks scope that work.
  </p>

  <h2>For agents</h2>
  <p>Read the specs over MCP rather than from files:</p>
  <pre><code>{`claude mcp add sysspec --scope project \\
  --env SPECS_DIR=/path/to/checkout/specs \\
  -- uvx --from sysspec sysspec-mcp`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Then <code>list_services()</code> → <code>get_service(name)</code> →
    narrow reads (<code>get_acceptance_criteria</code>,
    <code> get_message_schema</code>, <code>get_artifact</code>);
    <code> trace_channel(address)</code> shows who a change would break. The
    deeper processes live in the <code>implement-service</code> and{' '}
    <code>consume-service</code> skills
    (<a href={`https://github.com/${repoSlug}`}>{repoSlug}</a>).
  </p>
</StarlightPage>
